hexagon: fuse the LayerNorm and the attention input of the vision encoder

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus the patches before this one in patches/series.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp-vit-fusion.h      the match of the two chains, their placement checks, the op
                                                nodes and the allocation deps (a new file)
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp      the op names, the match in graph_compute, the deps in
                                                graph_optimize, the weight row of NORM_MUL_ADD in the unary
                                                params
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-ops.h         HTP_OP_NORM_MUL_ADD and HTP_OP_ROPE_QKV
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/unary-ops.h       the field has_bias, the VTCM rows of the weight and the bias
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/unary-ops.c       the task of NORM_MUL_ADD
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/rope-ops.h        the kernel params and the plan of ROPE_QKV
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/rope-ops.c        rope_ctx_init_params (the params of both ROPE ops) and the op
                                                ROPE_QKV
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/main.c, htp-ctx.h the dispatch of the two ops
    tests/test-backend-ops.cpp                  the test VIT_BLOCK, and the error of each compared tensor for a
                                                test that asks for it (print_err)

The commit of this patch also gives the two ops to the DSP model and to the symbolic check of the hexhost
harness (tests/fuzz/hexhost/common), and the graphs mode of tests/fuzz/hexhost/run.sh checks that the host
fuses the attention input of each layer of the vision encoder and each LayerNorm.

The time before this patch

The vision encoder of the 4B projector (24 layers, 2976 patches for 768 image tokens) on HTP0 spends 55.7 ms
in NORM, MUL and the bias ADD of its 49 LayerNorms, and 76.9 ms in the ROPE of Q and K and the two F16 casts
of K and V, of 559 ms of op time (the phone stage vit1, measured).

The change

1. HTP_OP_NORM_MUL_ADD. The chain ADD(MUL(NORM(x), w), b) of build_norm, with w and b one F32 row each,
   becomes one op of the unary kernel. The task calls norm_f32 of the NORM op on each block of rows in VTCM,
   then hvx_mul_f32_aaa of MUL and hvx_add_f32_aaa of ADD on each row. The weight row and the bias row go to
   the VTCM of each thread one time. The node of the op is the NORM node, thus the op params give its
   epsilon.

2. HTP_OP_ROPE_QKV. The chain ROPE(QK), CPY(the permuted key view of the ROPE output to F16), CPY(the permuted
   V view to F16) becomes one op. Each thread takes a range of tokens in blocks. A block of Q and K rows comes
   into VTCM with one DMA transfer, the op rotates them with the theta cache and hvx_rope_neox_f32_aa of the
   ROPE op, writes the ROPE output with DMA, and converts the key rows and the V rows with the conversion of
   the CPY op into the F16 rows of each head. The bits of the field io select DMA or HVX for V and for the
   F16 outputs; the preset value 3 moves each tensor with DMA, as the ROPE op does, with a ring of 4 buffer
   sets. The op needs an M-RoPE mode and v75 or later (the DMA strides of v73 have 16 bits).

3. The host matches the chains in graph_compute, after the gate chains. The fused op goes to the index of
   the last node of its chain. graph_optimize asks the scheduler to keep each input until that node, and the
   match checks the bytes of the allocated graph: no node between the two indexes writes a moved input or
   reads or writes a moved output, no other node reads a result that the fused op does not write, and no
   output shares bytes with an input. The key-view check of ROPE_QKV compares the view source and the view
   offset, because graph_optimize runs before ggml-alloc gives the tensors their data.

4. GGML_HEXAGON_FUSE_VIT holds the bits of the two plans (1 NORM_MUL_ADD, 2 ROPE_QKV, the preset value 3).
   GGML_HEXAGON_VIT_QKV_IO sets the field io (the preset value 3). GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION=0 disables the
   two plans.

5. The residual stream of the encoder has values of more than the F16 maximum (approximately 9e4 at the
   last layer of the 4B projector). Thus no fused op writes F16 other than the casts of K and V.

The tests on the host

- tests/fuzz/hexhost/run.sh graphs on the tree with this patch: the vision paths of the 2B and the 4B (576
  and 768 image tokens, 3 photos of 24 layers) give 72 ROPE_QKV, 147 NORM_MUL_ADD and 0 NORM each. The
  prefill 512 and the decode graphs of the 4B send the same ops as the tree without this patch, and the
  verbose log has no match line of the vision chains. The host time of one decode graph without the graph
  cache: 1839 to 1853 us before, 1843 to 1905 us with this patch.
- tests/fuzz/hexhost/run.sh test none --profile release with the DSP model and the symbolic check of the two
  ops: 0 findings for dirty, repack, mmap, envparse, kparams, graph and queue-cancel.

The results on the phone (HTP0 v79, OnePlus 13, the 4B Q8_0 and its projector; the stages vit4 and vit5 of
2026-09-25, measured)

- The embeddings have the bits of the graph without this patch for each input: the photo of the user at 768
  and 256 image tokens, and the older test photo at 768 and 256 tokens. Each plan alone has the same bits.
  Thus the NMSE against the naive x86 oracle does not change (7.25e-5 at 768 tokens).
- test-backend-ops: VIT_BLOCK 26 of 26, NORM, NORM_MUL_ADD, RMS_NORM, RMS_NORM_MUL_ADD, GELU and FFN_SWIGLU
  231 of 231, ROPE 287 of 287. The NMSE of the qkv cases of VIT_BLOCK is 1.1e-5 to 2.1e-5 for the io values
  0, 3 and 7 and for the plans off, thus no io value reads a stale byte.
- The op time of one encode at 768 tokens: 513 to 518 ms, against 578 to 591 ms without this patch. The
  LayerNorms take 19.5 ms (57 ms before), the attention input 32 to 35 ms (81 to 83 ms before). The QKV
  matmul takes 59 ms, against 44 to 47 ms before; with one plan alone it takes 41 to 47 ms.
- The warm encode: 507 ms against 599 ms at 768 tokens, 163 ms against 181 ms at 256 tokens.
- llama-bench of the text model, 3 alternated rounds (the median of the 2 rounds of each set that the cap
  filter keeps): pp512 1174 against 1164 t/s, tg32 11.97 against 11.98 t/s.

